# rule.require.enumerate-before-you-name

> list every instance the word must cover. THEN pick the word.

one instance hides both failures: **too narrow** (breaks on an unlisted case) and **too wide** (fits all, discriminates none).

test, before the word: 1. *what are ALL the instances it must cover?* 2. *does the candidate cover every one AND exclude its neighbours?*

row uncovered → too narrow: widen or split · covers every row and every adjacent concept → too wide: it is the genus, find the differentia · covers all, excludes neighbours → holds.

| when… | then… |
|---|---|
| you name a relation between two concepts | list every pair it must hold over |
| a word fits the one example in view | a sample of one; find the second |
| you reach for a term of art for precision | precision makes it narrow; check excluded rows |
| a candidate covers every instance | check adjacent concepts too |
| you cannot list a second instance | the concept may not earn a word yet |

blocker: a term proposed with one instance cited · an enumeration with an uncovered row · a term that covers every row and its neighbours.
false positive: a concept with genuinely one instance (say why).
